page guide_audio {
  title = "Guest audio"
  chapter_header "Guest audio" {
    kicker       = "The guide · explanation"
    reading_time = "3 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-31"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<'DOC'
A VM is silent unless you ask for sound. With `audio = true` the guest gets a
sound card, and what it plays comes out of your desktop like any application,
under a volume control of its own. This chapter covers turning it on, the
microphone, and where the sound goes. Audio needs vmlab-hv; a VM on QEMU has
no sound card (see [The host](guide_host)).
DOC

  h2 "Turn it on"

  p <<'DOC'
Set `audio = true` on the `vm` block, beside `hypervisor = "vmlab"` or on a
host whose VM default is vmlab-hv. Add `microphone = true` to hand the guest
your default microphone too. Both are off by default, and both belong to the
VM alone: a template or profile cannot turn them on.
DOC

  code wcl { filename = "vmlab.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab.wcl>

lab "media" {
  vm "desk" {
    template   = "x86_64/ubuntu-26.04"
    hypervisor = "vmlab"
    audio      = true
    microphone = true
  }
}
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
The guest sees an Intel HDA controller, which Windows and Linux drive with the
drivers they ship; nothing is installed in the guest. `vmlab validate` refuses
`audio` or `microphone` on a QEMU VM, naming vmlab-hv, and refuses
`microphone` without `audio`.
DOC

  h2 "Where the sound goes"

  p <<'DOC'
vmlab-hv plays the guest's sound straight into your audio session from its own
process: PipeWire when its socket is under `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, else the
PulseAudio server `PULSE_SERVER` names, which is how WSLg's audio is reached.
Each VM is its own node, `vmlab.<lab>.<machine>`, shown as
`vmlab <lab>/<machine>`, so `wpctl status` lists it and pavucontrol sets its
volume, mutes it or moves it to another output like any application.
DOC

  code shell { source = <<'SRC'
$ wpctl status | grep vmlab
 │      74. vmlab media/desk                    [vol: 1.00]
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
With `microphone = true` the card's capture stream records from your default
source. Without it no capture stream exists for that VM, so nothing in the
guest can listen. A host with neither PipeWire nor PulseAudio plays into a
silent sink, and the VM's `vmlab-hv.log` says so once.
DOC

  h2 "Snapshots and recordings"

  p <<'DOC'
An online snapshot of an audio VM restores with its sound resuming; at most
one period glitches as the host stream reopens. [Console recordings](guide_recording)
carry no audio.
DOC
}
